u.s president joe biden and his russian  counterpart vladimir putin will meet for  talks  in geneva as relations between the two  countries hit a low point  now biden arrived in the swiss city  saying he was ready for a tough  encounter with the russian president  he has promised to set clear red lines  between washington  and moscow but says he hopes there will  be areas for cooperation  the summit will be putin and biden's  first face-to-face meeting as  leaders and is expected to last for  several hours  let's get the latest on the story we  have dw's alexander phenomenon covering  the summit for us in geneva and yuri  rochetto is standing by  in moscow welcome to you both alexander  let's  start with you there in geneva so talk  us through the big points  on the agenda today  there is a full range of pressing issues  on the agenda according  to the white house you asked president  joe biden once  to talk about moscow's aggressive  actions in ukraine  he wants to call out russia for human  rights abuses  the poisoning of the opposition leader  alexey navalny  but i think when the leaders come  together at the villa la grange  right behind me it's a cyber security  that will be at the top  of the agenda there have been a few  significant far-reaching cyber attacks  in the u.s  lately some of them having russian  fingerprints on them  one targeted america's biggest fuel  pipeline so the us wants russia  to do more to deter to deter such  attacks from within its voters  well you know alexander just a few  months a week ago we saw the u.s  president say when he was asked that  vladimir putin is a killer he's talked  about drawing red lines in this meeting  so  how tense do you think the summit is  going to be  well fact is that the relations between  the two countries  are at a very low point and the us  president joe biden he has made clear  that  he wants to be tough on russia however  my impression is  that he's also interested in lowering  the temperature a notch or two and  white house officials told us that the  main goal here is to find areas where  both  sides can work together on climate  change for example on solving  international crises in libya for  example or  in syria the big question of course is  whether vladimir putin will play along  yuri that brings us to you there in  moscow this uh effort perhaps to  to bring temperatures down a bit i mean  we know that president biden will  confront  president putin with these allegations  that russian hackers have been behind  these big cyber attacks so  from your perspective there in moscow  how contentious do you think this is  going to be  well some are pretty contentious i think  but some progress could be achieved as  well at this point because ahead of the  summit  both moscow and washington have already  made some steps  that might seem unusual against the  backdrop of tensions in recent years  they've tried to smooth some of the  sharper edges of the relationship  avoid unnecessary signals that could  escalate the situation and they even  exchanged already  what could be called some kind of  compliment the american president said  that he is that he perceives his russian  colleague as a worthy rival and vladimir  putin here in moscow for his part spoke  very positively  about biden's team praising them for  their professionalism in an interview  tv interview when asked about cyber  attacks against the us and  the possible extradition to the united  states of russian hackers  who have committed those cyber attacks  president putin admitted in an interview  it was a possibility once appropriate  agreements have been reached with  washington  that was a kind of signal to the white  house indicating the kremlin's goodwill  and readiness for dialogue at this point  and yuri just quickly what do you think  president putin wants to get  out of this meeting  a direct a direct dialogue between the  two men that's exactly what  the russian president said he wants to  establish russian-american relations are  probably already at their lowest point  since  the collapse of the soviet union from  here they can only get better  although both sides are warning against  high expectations  still the importance of this meeting  shouldn't be underestimated  the main thing now is to try to start  salvaging relations from their current  level and prevent a direct possibly  armed clash  between the two nuclear countries such  as scenario sumi has become more and  more realistic given the way things have  developed in recent years  first of all because russia and the  united states had stopped talking  altogether and stage  not reached even during the cold war and  both sides probably have a weak  understanding of where the so-called  so-called red lines are those lines that  shouldn't be crossed under any  circumstances  so here in moscow they're convinced that  neither of the two presidents will shake  hands for just for the record  putin wants to achieve results even if  that only means resuming  the bilateral bilateral dialogue under  today's conditions even that would be a  lot  our correspondent siri rochette there in  moscow alexander fornamen in  geneva thank you
 

